  "convention": "Old facets0..11, first S-cut new facet12=a, second T-cut new facet13=b. Equivalent order: T introduces b, then {b} union (S minus T) introduces a.",
  "proof": "If T is not contained in S, the first missing face S survives. If T is a proper subset of S, the displayed vertex identity holds. Since S was an old face, T and {b} union (S minus T) are both minimal nonfaces of the final complex: no proper subset can contain an old missing face, and the new facet generators cannot shorten either support. Thus a final compact Coxeter nerve forces their orders at most5. A second cut of order>5 is itself a surviving large missing face. All admissible two-cut recipes therefore normalize to two cuts of codimensions2..5.",
  "scope": "Every simple H9 polytope on12 facets and two proper face truncations of codimensions2..9. The finite check confirms the three universal vertex cases for every cardinality; it does not enumerate arbitrary simple polytopes. Together with the written minimal-nonface argument this extends the complete Bugaenko wedge/two-small-cuts exclusion to all two-face truncations after that wedge."
